[candidate · 1:18] Here's how I'd approach it. Since revenue is up 15% but margin collapsed from 24% to 6%, the problem is almost certainly on the cost side, but I want to confirm that before I commit.

So first, I'd decompose profit into revenue and costs. On revenue, I'd check price versus volume versus mix — specifically whether the 15% growth came from opening new stores or from same-store sales, because new-store growth can carry heavy fixed costs and drag margin even while topline looks healthy.

Second, costs. I'd split fixed from variable. Variable would be coffee beans, milk, cups, and store labor. Fixed would be rent, corporate overhead, marketing, and any depreciation from new buildouts.

Third, I'd look at which cost line grew fastest relative to revenue over the two years — that isolates the driver.

Could I start with the store count: how many of the 200 locations opened in the last two years?
