[candidate · 3:56] COGS is the driver, and the numbers close cleanly. COGS up 16 points, overhead up 2, labor flat — that's 18 points, which is exactly the margin decline from 24% to 6%. So COGS explains about 89% of the gap and I'd focus there.

Next I'd break COGS into price per unit versus quantity per unit sold. Three possibilities. One, input cost inflation — green coffee prices, dairy, or packaging rose and we didn't pass it through. Two, waste or shrinkage — more product thrown out per drink, which would show up as higher quantity per transaction. Three, product mix — if we've shifted toward higher-cost items like oat milk lattes or food, COGS percentage rises even at constant margins per unit.

So I'd want the input cost index for beans and dairy over the two years, and the mix of units sold by category. Since labor is flat as a share of revenue, that also tells me volume is probably tracking revenue, which nudges me toward a price or mix story rather than waste.
