[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 4:20] Okay, you said the trend extrapolates to a loss within a year and that price has to carry most of the fix. Beyond price increases, what else could Brew & Bean do to reverse the margin decline? Give me as wide a range of ideas as you can.

[candidate · 4:29] Let me go wide across four buckets.

On procurement: lock in forward contracts or hedge green coffee rather than buying spot; consolidate suppliers for volume discounts; dual-source origins to reduce exposure to one region; renegotiate dairy and packaging, where 37.5% inflation looks more negotiable than a global commodity.

On product and mix: shift promotion toward lower-COGS, higher-margin items — brewed drip and espresso carry far better unit economics than milk-heavy or food items; introduce a premium tier that prices above inflation; charge separately for oat and alternative milks, which many chains already do.

On portion and waste: audit pour sizes and shot weights, since even small overpour at 58% COGS is expensive; tighten end-of-day food par levels; cut low-velocity SKUs that spoil.

On revenue quality: loyalty programs and subscriptions to protect volume if we raise prices; raise attach rate on food, which lifts ticket; targeted price increases by market rather than uniform.

On overhead: the two-point drift deserves a look — it's small but it's moving the wrong way.

Honestly, though, price is the only lever sized to an 18-point gap.
