[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 4:31] Okay, a broad set of ideas, with price as the main lever. Suppose you're in front of the CEO right now. What do you recommend Brew & Bean do, and what is the first step?

[candidate · 4:38] My recommendation: Brew & Bean should raise menu prices, phased, starting immediately — targeting roughly 15–16% in total, paired with supply contracts and mix changes to cover the rest.

The reasoning. COGS is 89% of the margin problem, driven entirely by 37.5–40% input inflation that we never passed through in two years. No other lever is sized to an 18-point gap: labor has been disciplined at 22% the whole time, and overhead is only two points. Roughly, a 15% price increase on flat volume takes COGS from 58% back to about 50% of revenue, recovering eight points of margin; forward-contracting beans and shifting mix toward drip and espresso closes much of the remainder.

Risks: volume is our only growth engine right now, so elasticity is the real danger — and we may have attracted price-sensitive traffic precisely because we held prices while competitors moved.

First step: a four-week price test in 20 stores, two increments, measuring transaction volume and ticket. That gives us an elasticity read before we touch the other 180.
